import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    static char[] letters;
    static boolean[] used;
    static char[] current;
    static StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();

    static void build(int depth) {
        if (depth == letters.length) {
            out.append(new String(current)).append('\n');
            return;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < letters.length; i++) {
            if (used[i]) continue;
            used[i] = true;
            current[depth] = letters[i];
            build(depth + 1);
            used[i] = false;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        letters = new char[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) letters[i] = sc.next().charAt(0);
        Arrays.sort(letters);
        used = new boolean[n];
        current = new char[n];
        build(0);
        System.out.print(out.toString());
    }
}

lesson:
# Pruning

A correct backtracker can still time out. **Pruning** cuts branches that can't yield a solution.

```java
Arrays.sort(a);
void go(int start, int remaining) {
    if (remaining == 0) { count++; return; }
    for (int i = start; i < a.length; i++) {
        if (a[i] > remaining) break;      // sorted → nothing later fits
        if (i > start && a[i] == a[i-1]) continue; // skip duplicate choice
        go(i, remaining - a[i]);
    }
}
```

## Three standard cuts
| Cut | Why it's safe |
|---|---|
| sort + `break` when candidate > remaining | everything after is even bigger |
| skip `a[i]==a[i-1]` at the same depth | avoids generating a duplicate result |
| bound check before recursing | prune impossible sub-trees early |

